{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgvuxfjnxAdYgzFcRDPYD","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01LHiTijF4gSMysiKjcYrhY4","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /workspace; grep -n \"Reference and baselines\" paper.tex; awk '/Reference and baselines/{f=1} f' paper.tex | head -40 | sed -n 1,40p | cut -c1-3000; grep -n -i \"paper_data\\|ablation\" paper.tex | head"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4285,"cache_read_input_tokens":57505,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4285},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"227ca7a1-03f6-48a6-8e88-6408f1674ca6","uuid":"3a3ac9ea-21ac-4dfa-9f8e-1bf9f4028aab","timestamp":"2026-11-11T09:35:55.648Z","request_id":"req_011CgvuxfBJa8GAZnN9siXLW"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LHiTijF4gSMysiKjcYrhY4","type":"tool_result","content":"208:\\paragraph{Reference and baselines.} The reference solver is exact only for some rules, so efficiency is relative, not optimal; a value above 1 is possible and was observed on average. Efficiency is capped at 2 per episode, which bounds the influence of individual cases, and costs under 0.01 points are floored, which hides tie-breaking differences. The planner was not compared with generic integer-programming or black-box optimization baselines other than the MILP variant on the development data.\n\\paragraph{Reference and baselines.} The reference solver is exact only for some rules, so efficiency is relative, not optimal; a value above 1 is possible and was observed on average. Efficiency is capped at 2 per episode, which bounds the influence of individual cases, and costs under 0.01 points are floored, which hides tie-breaking differences. The planner was not compared with generic integer-programming or black-box optimization baselines other than the MILP variant on the development data.\n\n\\paragraph{Statistics.} Two evaluator seeds on two boards that share a model pool, and two seeds on one development leaderboard, are the only variability evidence. Differences among configurations in the third decimal should be read as descriptive. Candidate-reported diagnostics are unverified.\n\n\\paragraph{What a rule-dependence test would require.} Answering the motivating question needs paired, per-case costs rather than efficiencies: for each board, target rank and cost measure, the reference (or planner) cost under every rule, including infeasible cases as censored outcomes rather than zeros. Rule effects would then be summarized with bootstrap intervals over targets and compared with paired rank tests with multiplicity correction; the shuffled-score null would indicate how much of any difference comes from the correlation structure of real task scores rather than from the rule itself; and sensitivity to the field size, the cap policy and the target rank would be reported as robustness checks. The planner source contains offline helpers for the interval and test computations, but none of them was run to produce a reported number.\n\n\\paragraph{Coupling between planner and evaluator.} The planner's internal rule replica implements the same definitions that the task specifies and that the evaluator's rule engine uses, so agreement between the two on whether a plan succeeds is by construction; the evaluator nevertheless recomputes every plan with its own independent code, and a disagreement would have scored zero. The retained evidence does not report any such disagreement, but it also does not break out failure counts, other than the plan validity rate of 1.0, which only covers well-formedness.\n\n\\paragraph{Dual use.} A tool that finds cheap rank-lifting interventions could support gaming of leaderboards. We view it instead as a diagnostic for benchmark designers: an aggregation rule whose rank is cheaply reachable by few-task interventions is a warning sign, though this paper does not measure that for any rule.\n\n\\section{Conclusion}\nWe formalized targeted investment on multi-task leaderboards and evaluated a verification-first planner that returns only plans re-checked against an exact replica of the aggregation rule. On two held-out Open LLM Leaderboard boards, the planner's mean efficiency relative to an evaluator-owned reference solver was 1.0032, against 0.8166 for the better naive baseline, with a plan validity rate of 1.0 and a qualified outcome. A development comparison on HELM MMLU suggests that boundary polishing improves cost slightly (combined 1.00339 versus 1.00183 for the base solvers) at higher compute, whereas an additional MILP level-selection layer (with a z-score exchange polish) changed nothing measurable. These conclusions are conditional on the declared investment model, rest on two evaluator seeds, and concern planner cost-efficiency. The central question that motivated the study, how much required investment varies with the aggregation rule, needs per-rule, per-target cost tables with paired uncertainty and the shuffled-null comparison; these remain future work, as do models with spillover and heterogeneous task costs.\n\n\\paragraph{Reproducibility statement.} The planner sources, the evaluator sources, the task contract and the script that builds each figure are included with the submission (Appendix~\\ref{app:repro}). Data snapshots are identified by revision hash and file checksums. All reported numbers are read from evaluator outputs.\n\n\\paragraph{Ethics statement.} The work uses only public leaderboard scores and trains no models. Dual-use considerations are discussed above.\n\n% IMPORTANT: \\clearpage flushes all pending floats before references so that\n% body-page counting excludes reference-only pages.  Do NOT remove.\n\\clearpage\n\\bibliography{references}\n\\bibliographystyle{paper}\n\n\\clearpage\n\\appendix\n\\section{Supplementary Information}\n\n\\subsection{Investment model, rules and elementary cost identities}\\label{app:model}\n\\paragraph{Instance definition.} An instance is a tuple $(X,i,r,\\text{cost model})$ with $X\\in[0,100]^{N\\times T}$ restricted to the field, a target row $i$, a rule $r$ from Table~\\ref{tab:rules}, and L1 or L0 costs. Per-task headroom is $h_t=c_t-x_{it}$. Rule definitions follow the evaluator's rule engine: Borda scores are minus the mean per-task rank (ties share half credit), plurality counts tasks on which a model is the unique maximizer, Copeland counts pairwise task-majority wins with half credit for ties, and the trimmed mean drops $\\lfloor T/5\\rfloor$ tasks from each end. The main cases are the 32 combinations of rule, target rank (2, 10) and cost measure per board; control cases use rank 2 and L1 for each of the 8 rules, once for the permuted input and once for the shuffled null.\n\n\\begin{proposition}[Mean and maximin]\nLet $\\Delta=\\max_{j\\ne i}\\bar x_j-\\bar x_i$ with $\\bar x$ the row mean, and $m^\\star=\\max_{j\\neq i}\\min_t x_{jt}$. Under the mean, an L1 plan succeeds iff $\\sum_t g_t>T\\Delta$, so the infimum cost is $T\\Delta$ (attained up to the margin) provided $\\sum_t h_t>T\\Delta$; the L0 cost is the smallest $k$ such that the $k$ largest headrooms sum to more than $T\\Delta$. Under maximin, success requires $\\min_t(x_{it}+g_t)>m^\\star$, so the L1 infimum is $\\sum_t\\max(0,m^\\star-x_{it})$ and the L0 cost is $|\\{t:x_{it}\\le m^\\star\\}|$, subject to the caps.\n\\end{proposition}\n\\emph{Proof sketch.} The mean of the modified row is $\\bar x_i+\\sum_t g_t/T$ and rivals are unchanged, which gives the first claim; allocation across tasks is irrelevant for L1, and for L0 the cheapest set of tasks is the one with the largest headroom. For maximin, every task with $x_{it}\\le m^\\star$ must be lifted above $m^\\star$ and no other task needs to move. \\hfill$\\square$\n\nThese identities are elementary consequences of the definitions and agree with the closed forms documented in the planner source; they are not used to produce any reported number. The remaining rules lack comparably simple identities, which is why the planner relies on verified search.\n\n\\subsection{Full retained metrics}\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n150:The held-out evaluation completed and its outcome is qualified. The planner's main efficiency is 1.0032 relative to the reference solver, % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.main_efficiency]]\n151:versus 0.8166 for the better naive baseline per case, % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.baseline_main_efficiency]]\n152:a difference of 0.187 (Figure~\\ref{fig:final}a). The plan validity rate is 1.0, % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.plan_validity_rate]]\n153:so no episode was malformed, timed out or out of bounds. Control efficiency is 1.0002, % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.control_efficiency]]\n154:and the combined score is 1.0026. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.combined_score]]\n155:Per-seed combined scores were 1.00260 and 1.00257 for seeds 9001 and 9002, % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\n158:Three observations bound the interpretation. First, an efficiency near 1 means that the planner's verified plans cost, on average, about the same as the reference plans; the retained value does not reveal how many cases sit exactly at 1, how many are above, or whether any case is below. Second, the planner's candidate set includes the uniform-gain and headroom-fill plans, and the comparison is between the whole frozen planner (base solvers, verification, pruning, boundary polishing and the uniform-gain fallback) and the evaluator's naive baselines. No held-out ablation separates the per-rule solvers from those other components, so the gap of 0.187 cannot be attributed to the solvers specifically (in development, polishing alone moved main efficiency from 1.0031 to 1.0051). The comparison also does not show that this planner is better than other sophisticated planners, which were not evaluated. Third, the two evaluator seeds do not resample the leaderboard: they change permutation and shuffle draws and restart randomness, so the 3.5$\\times10^{-5}$ spread between seeds reflects solver and control variability, not data sampling, and no confidence interval or significance test can be formed from two values.\n175:and combined score 1.00339, an increase of 0.0016 in combined score over Base (Figure~\\ref{fig:search}). % @evidence[[paper_data.json#all_programs]]\n191:H1: frozen planner beats naive baselines & Supported for the whole planner & Held-out main efficiency 1.0032 vs.\\ 0.8166; two boards, two seeds; no held-out ablation isolates the per-rule solvers; pool includes the naive plan\\\\\n255:Main efficiency (planner) & 1.0031855 \\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.main_efficiency]]","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"227ca7a1-03f6-48a6-8e88-6408f1674ca6","uuid":"ee783790-d24e-431b-b0df-c542d4d00f64","timestamp":"2026-11-11T09:35:55.721Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"208:\\paragraph{Reference and baselines.} The reference solver is exact only for some rules, so efficiency is relative, not optimal; a value above 1 is possible and was observed on average. Efficiency is capped at 2 per episode, which bounds the influence of individual cases, and costs under 0.01 points are floored, which hides tie-breaking differences. The planner was not compared with generic integer-programming or black-box optimization baselines other than the MILP variant on the development data.\n\\paragraph{Reference and baselines.} The reference solver is exact only for some rules, so efficiency is relative, not optimal; a value above 1 is possible and was observed on average. Efficiency is capped at 2 per episode, which bounds the influence of individual cases, and costs under 0.01 points are floored, which hides tie-breaking differences. The planner was not compared with generic integer-programming or black-box optimization baselines other than the MILP variant on the development data.\n\n\\paragraph{Statistics.} Two evaluator seeds on two boards that share a model pool, and two seeds on one development leaderboard, are the only variability evidence. Differences among configurations in the third decimal should be read as descriptive. Candidate-reported diagnostics are unverified.\n\n\\paragraph{What a rule-dependence test would require.} Answering the motivating question needs paired, per-case costs rather than efficiencies: for each board, target rank and cost measure, the reference (or planner) cost under every rule, including infeasible cases as censored outcomes rather than zeros. Rule effects would then be summarized with bootstrap intervals over targets and compared with paired rank tests with multiplicity correction; the shuffled-score null would indicate how much of any difference comes from the correlation structure of real task scores rather than from the rule itself; and sensitivity to the field size, the cap policy and the target rank would be reported as robustness checks. The planner source contains offline helpers for the interval and test computations, but none of them was run to produce a reported number.\n\n\\paragraph{Coupling between planner and evaluator.} The planner's internal rule replica implements the same definitions that the task specifies and that the evaluator's rule engine uses, so agreement between the two on whether a plan succeeds is by construction; the evaluator nevertheless recomputes every plan with its own independent code, and a disagreement would have scored zero. The retained evidence does not report any such disagreement, but it also does not break out failure counts, other than the plan validity rate of 1.0, which only covers well-formedness.\n\n\\paragraph{Dual use.} A tool that finds cheap rank-lifting interventions could support gaming of leaderboards. We view it instead as a diagnostic for benchmark designers: an aggregation rule whose rank is cheaply reachable by few-task interventions is a warning sign, though this paper does not measure that for any rule.\n\n\\section{Conclusion}\nWe formalized targeted investment on multi-task leaderboards and evaluated a verification-first planner that returns only plans re-checked against an exact replica of the aggregation rule. On two held-out Open LLM Leaderboard boards, the planner's mean efficiency relative to an evaluator-owned reference solver was 1.0032, against 0.8166 for the better naive baseline, with a plan validity rate of 1.0 and a qualified outcome. A development comparison on HELM MMLU suggests that boundary polishing improves cost slightly (combined 1.00339 versus 1.00183 for the base solvers) at higher compute, whereas an additional MILP level-selection layer (with a z-score exchange polish) changed nothing measurable. These conclusions are conditional on the declared investment model, rest on two evaluator seeds, and concern planner cost-efficiency. The central question that motivated the study, how much required investment varies with the aggregation rule, needs per-rule, per-target cost tables with paired uncertainty and the shuffled-null comparison; these remain future work, as do models with spillover and heterogeneous task costs.\n\n\\paragraph{Reproducibility statement.} The planner sources, the evaluator sources, the task contract and the script that builds each figure are included with the submission (Appendix~\\ref{app:repro}). Data snapshots are identified by revision hash and file checksums. All reported numbers are read from evaluator outputs.\n\n\\paragraph{Ethics statement.} The work uses only public leaderboard scores and trains no models. Dual-use considerations are discussed above.\n\n% IMPORTANT: \\clearpage flushes all pending floats before references so that\n% body-page counting excludes reference-only pages.  Do NOT remove.\n\\clearpage\n\\bibliography{references}\n\\bibliographystyle{paper}\n\n\\clearpage\n\\appendix\n\\section{Supplementary Information}\n\n\\subsection{Investment model, rules and elementary cost identities}\\label{app:model}\n\\paragraph{Instance definition.} An instance is a tuple $(X,i,r,\\text{cost model})$ with $X\\in[0,100]^{N\\times T}$ restricted to the field, a target row $i$, a rule $r$ from Table~\\ref{tab:rules}, and L1 or L0 costs. Per-task headroom is $h_t=c_t-x_{it}$. Rule definitions follow the evaluator's rule engine: Borda scores are minus the mean per-task rank (ties share half credit), plurality counts tasks on which a model is the unique maximizer, Copeland counts pairwise task-majority wins with half credit for ties, and the trimmed mean drops $\\lfloor T/5\\rfloor$ tasks from each end. The main cases are the 32 combinations of rule, target rank (2, 10) and cost measure per board; control cases use rank 2 and L1 for each of the 8 rules, once for the permuted input and once for the shuffled null.\n\n\\begin{proposition}[Mean and maximin]\nLet $\\Delta=\\max_{j\\ne i}\\bar x_j-\\bar x_i$ with $\\bar x$ the row mean, and $m^\\star=\\max_{j\\neq i}\\min_t x_{jt}$. Under the mean, an L1 plan succeeds iff $\\sum_t g_t>T\\Delta$, so the infimum cost is $T\\Delta$ (attained up to the margin) provided $\\sum_t h_t>T\\Delta$; the L0 cost is the smallest $k$ such that the $k$ largest headrooms sum to more than $T\\Delta$. Under maximin, success requires $\\min_t(x_{it}+g_t)>m^\\star$, so the L1 infimum is $\\sum_t\\max(0,m^\\star-x_{it})$ and the L0 cost is $|\\{t:x_{it}\\le m^\\star\\}|$, subject to the caps.\n\\end{proposition}\n\\emph{Proof sketch.} The mean of the modified row is $\\bar x_i+\\sum_t g_t/T$ and rivals are unchanged, which gives the first claim; allocation across tasks is irrelevant for L1, and for L0 the cheapest set of tasks is the one with the largest headroom. For maximin, every task with $x_{it}\\le m^\\star$ must be lifted above $m^\\star$ and no other task needs to move. \\hfill$\\square$\n\nThese identities are elementary consequences of the definitions and agree with the closed forms documented in the planner source; they are not used to produce any reported number. The remaining rules lack comparably simple identities, which is why the planner relies on verified search.\n\n\\subsection{Full retained metrics}\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n150:The held-out evaluation completed and its outcome is qualified. The planner's main efficiency is 1.0032 relative to the reference solver, % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.main_efficiency]]\n151:versus 0.8166 for the better naive baseline per case, % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.baseline_main_efficiency]]\n152:a difference of 0.187 (Figure~\\ref{fig:final}a). The plan validity rate is 1.0, % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.plan_validity_rate]]\n153:so no episode was malformed, timed out or out of bounds. Control efficiency is 1.0002, % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.control_efficiency]]\n154:and the combined score is 1.0026. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.combined_score]]\n155:Per-seed combined scores were 1.00260 and 1.00257 for seeds 9001 and 9002, % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\n158:Three observations bound the interpretation. First, an efficiency near 1 means that the planner's verified plans cost, on average, about the same as the reference plans; the retained value does not reveal how many cases sit exactly at 1, how many are above, or whether any case is below. Second, the planner's candidate set includes the uniform-gain and headroom-fill plans, and the comparison is between the whole frozen planner (base solvers, verification, pruning, boundary polishing and the uniform-gain fallback) and the evaluator's naive baselines. No held-out ablation separates the per-rule solvers from those other components, so the gap of 0.187 cannot be attributed to the solvers specifically (in development, polishing alone moved main efficiency from 1.0031 to 1.0051). The comparison also does not show that this planner is better than other sophisticated planners, which were not evaluated. Third, the two evaluator seeds do not resample the leaderboard: they change permutation and shuffle draws and restart randomness, so the 3.5$\\times10^{-5}$ spread between seeds reflects solver and control variability, not data sampling, and no confidence interval or significance test can be formed from two values.\n175:and combined score 1.00339, an increase of 0.0016 in combined score over Base (Figure~\\ref{fig:search}). % @evidence[[paper_data.json#all_programs]]\n191:H1: frozen planner beats naive baselines & Supported for the whole planner & Held-out main efficiency 1.0032 vs.\\ 0.8166; two boards, two seeds; no held-out ablation isolates the per-rule solvers; pool includes the naive plan\\\\\n255:Main efficiency (planner) & 1.0031855 \\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.main_efficiency]]","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
